CH = dict(num=11, title="Earth, Moon, and the Sun", theme="Rotation, revolution, seasons, solar and lunar eclipses",
intro="Rashmika in Kanniyakumari notices coconut-tree shadows are long in the morning and short at noon, and wonders: does the Sun move, or does the Earth? The chapter explains the Earth's rotation (day and night, apparent motion of the Sun, Moon and stars), its revolution (changing night sky, seasons due to tilt), and how the Sun, Moon and Earth line up to cause solar and lunar eclipses.",
big_questions=["Why does the Sun rise in the East?", "What causes day and night?", "Why do seasons occur?", "How can a small Moon hide the huge Sun?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "12.1 Rotation of the Earth"),
("p", "The Sun appears to rise in the East, cross the sky and set in the West. **Merry-go-round activity (12.1):** when you turn anti-clockwise on a merry-go-round, objects around you appear to turn the opposite way (clockwise). In the same way, the Sun *appears* to move because we view it from a turning Earth."),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Rotation** is the motion in which all parts of an object move in circles around an imaginary line through it, the **axis of rotation**. Examples: a spinning top, fan or ball."),
("ul", ["The Earth spins on its axis, which passes through the geographic **North and South Poles**.", "One rotation takes about **24 hours**.", "Seen from above the North Pole, the Earth turns **anti-clockwise, i.e. from West to East**."]),
("steps", [("Globe activity (12.2)", "Mark your location with a sticker on a globe and turn it anti-clockwise as seen from above the North Pole until the sticker returns to its place."), ("Add a torch", "Shine a torch from about 1.5 m. Half the globe is lit (day) and half dark (night)."), ("Check direction", "Light falls on the eastern part of India first only when the globe turns West to East."), ("Observe", "As your location turns into light there is sunrise; turning into darkness is sunset.")]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Day and night** are caused by the Earth's rotation: the side facing the Sun has day, the other side night."),
("h3", "What an observer on the rotating Earth sees"),
("p", "Morning: the Sun appears on the East side. Noon: Sun overhead. Evening: it disappears in the West and stars appear. Hence the Sun, and also the **Moon**, appear to rise in the East and set in the West."),
("h3", "Apparent motion of stars (Activity 12.3)"),
("ul", ["Draw the position of the Big Dipper (Saptarishi) relative to the Pole Star every two hours on the same night. It appears to move around the **Pole Star (Dhruva Tara)**.", "The Earth's axis points almost at the Pole Star, so it looks nearly **stationary** while all stars seem to circle it. Long-exposure photos show star trails as circular arcs.", "**Foucault pendulum:** Leon Foucault (mid-19th century) used a long pendulum to demonstrate the Earth's rotation. A 22 m Foucault pendulum hangs in the Constitution Hall of the new Parliament building in New Delhi.", "**Aryabhata** (*Aryabhatiya*, about 5th century CE) explained the stars' apparent motion with a boat analogy and gave the rotation period as about 23 h 56 min 4.1 s, very close to the modern value."]),
("h2", "12.2 Revolution of the Earth"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Revolution** is the motion of an object around another object. The path it follows is its **orbit**. The Earth's orbit around the Sun is nearly circular when seen from above. The Earth completes one revolution in about **365 days 6 hours** (about one year)."),
("table", ["", "Rotation", "Revolution"], [["Motion", "Spinning on own axis", "Going around the Sun"], ["Time taken", "About 24 hours", "About 365¼ days"], ["Causes", "Day and night; apparent motion of Sun, Moon and stars", "Changing night sky through the year; (with tilt) seasons"]]),
("h3", "12.2.1 Changing view of the night sky"),
("p", "Because the Earth moves around the Sun, the direction we look at in the night sky after sunset changes through the year, so **different stars and constellations are seen in different months**. The Bhil and Pawara people of the Tapi Valley used star patterns as markers for the arrival of monsoon."),
("h3", "12.2.2 Seasons on the Earth"),
("p", "The Earth's axis is **tilted** relative to its orbit and keeps the same tilt (pointing the same way) throughout the year. The **tilt and the spherical shape** of the Earth cause seasons."),
("table", ["", "June (Northern Hemisphere)", "December (Northern Hemisphere)"], [["Tilt", "Northern Hemisphere tilted **towards** the Sun", "Northern Hemisphere tilted **away** from the Sun"], ["Sunrays", "Same rays fall on a smaller area, so **more intense**", "Spread over a larger area, so **less intense**"], ["Day length", "More than 12 hours", "Less than 12 hours"], ["Season", "**Summer**", "**Winter**"], ["Southern Hemisphere", "Winter", "Summer"], ["North Pole", "24 hours of sunlight", "24 hours of darkness"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Wrong reasons:** seasons are *not* due to the Earth being closer to the Sun when a hemisphere tilts towards it, nor to the oval orbit. The distance difference is tiny; in fact the Earth is closest to the Sun in **January**."),
("table", ["Date (Northern Hemisphere)", "Name", "Day length"], [["About 21 June", "Summer solstice", "Longest day"], ["About 22 December", "Winter solstice", "Shortest day, longest night"], ["About 21 March", "Spring equinox", "12 hours"], ["About 23 September", "Autumn equinox", "12 hours"]]),
("ul", ["At the **North Pole** the Sun rises on 21 March and stays up about six months; it sets about 22 September. The South Pole is opposite: six months of sunshine then six months of darkness.", "At the **equator** there are always 12 hours of daylight and 12 hours of darkness, and the Sun's intensity hardly varies, so **southern India** feels less marked seasons.", "Local geography and nearness to seas also influence seasons.", "If the Earth's axis were not tilted there would be **no seasons** as we know them."]),
("h2", "12.3 Eclipses"),
("p", "Mercury and Venus, though between the Earth and Sun, look too small to block the Sun. When Venus passes between the Earth and Sun it appears as a tiny dot (a rare **Transit of Venus**). The **Moon**, a natural satellite of the Earth that revolves around it, can block the Sun."),
("h3", "12.3.1 Solar eclipse"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "A **solar eclipse** occurs when the Moon comes between the Sun and the Earth and blocks sunlight from reaching a part of the Earth."),
("ul", ["**Apparent size** depends on actual size and distance. A thumb at arm's length can hide a friend's head 5 m away (Activity 12.4). The Moon is much smaller than the Sun but much **closer**, so both look about the same size in the sky.", "In the Moon's shadow (a small area on Earth) the Sun is wholly hidden: **total solar eclipse**, when the sky darkens for a few minutes. Where the Moon covers only part of the Sun the observer sees a **partial solar eclipse**.", "The Moon's shadow moves across the Earth because of the Earth's rotation and the Moon's motion, so totality lasts only a few minutes. A 'diamond ring' effect appears just as the Moon moves away."]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Safe viewing:** never look at the Sun directly during an eclipse; it can cause blindness. Sunglasses, binoculars and telescopes are NOT safe. Use solar-eclipse goggles, project the Sun's image with a mirror (teacher-supervised; never reflect light into anyone's eyes), or attend planetarium or astronomy-club viewing events."),
("ul", ["Earlier people feared eclipses and held superstitions (not eating, cooking or going out). Now we know the cause, we need not fear them. Eclipses give scientists a chance to study the Sun.", "An eclipse is called **grahan** in Sanskrit and many Indian languages. The *Surya Siddhanta* gives calculations to predict eclipses."]),
("h3", "12.3.2 Lunar eclipse"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "A **lunar eclipse** occurs when the Earth comes between the Sun and the Moon and its shadow falls on the Moon, blocking sunlight from reaching it."),
("table", ["", "Solar eclipse", "Lunar eclipse"], [["Order of bodies", "Sun &ndash; Moon &ndash; Earth", "Sun &ndash; Earth &ndash; Moon"], ["What blocks light", "Moon", "Earth"], ["Shadow falls on", "A small part of the Earth", "The Moon"], ["Types", "Total, partial", "Total (Moon appears dark red), partial"], ["Visible from", "Only a small region of Earth", "Anywhere on the night side of Earth"], ["Safe to see with naked eye?", "**No**", "Yes"], ["Duration", "A few minutes (total)", "Much longer"]]),
("p", "**Useful facts:** the free Stellarium app tells about upcoming eclipses. **Kodaikanal Solar Observatory** (Palani hills, est. 1899, run by the Indian Institute of Astrophysics, Bengaluru) has Sun data for 100+ years. **M. K. Vainu Bappu**, father of modern Indian astronomy, set up telescopes at Manora Peak (Nainital) and Kavalur (the observatory is named after him), discovered a comet and studied eclipses."),
("h2", "Likely exam questions"),
("qa", [("Why does the Sun appear to rise in the East and set in the West?", "The Earth rotates from West to East."),
("Define rotation and axis of rotation.", "Rotation: all parts move in circles about a line through the object; that line is the axis."),
("What causes day and night?", "The Earth's rotation on its axis in about 24 hours."),
("Why does the Pole Star appear stationary?", "The Earth's axis points almost at it."),
("Define revolution and orbit. How long is one revolution of the Earth?", "Motion around another object; the path is the orbit; about 365 days 6 hours."),
("Why are different stars seen in different months?", "The Earth revolves around the Sun, so we face different directions of space after sunset."),
("What causes seasons?", "The tilt of the Earth's axis with its spherical shape, causing different sunray intensity and day lengths."),
("Why is it summer in the Northern Hemisphere in June?", "It is tilted towards the Sun, getting more intense light for more than 12 hours."),
("Is the Earth closer to the Sun in summer?", "No; the Earth is closest in January."),
("Why are seasons less marked in southern India?", "Near the equator day length and sun intensity change little."),
("Define solstice and equinox.", "Solstice: longest (21 June) or shortest (22 December) day; equinox: about 21 March and 23 September with equal day and night."),
("How can the small Moon cover the large Sun?", "It is much closer, so apparent sizes are similar."),
("Differentiate a solar and a lunar eclipse.", "Solar: Moon between Sun and Earth; lunar: Earth between Sun and Moon."),
("Why is it unsafe to view a solar eclipse directly?", "Sunlight can damage the eyes and cause blindness, even with sunglasses."),
("Why is a lunar eclipse visible from a larger area than a total solar eclipse?", "The Earth's shadow covers the whole Moon, seen by all on the night side; the Moon's shadow on Earth is small."),
("Should the Indian cricket team pack winter or summer clothes for Australia in December?", "Summer clothes; December is summer in the Southern Hemisphere.")]),
("h2", "Exploratory projects"),
("ul", ["Repeat the globe activity with an electric lamp and a tilted globe at four positions on a circle to see how much of each hemisphere is lit and how day length changes.", "Draw circles of radius 14.7 cm and 15.2 cm (1 cm = 10 million km) to see how small the Earth's closest and farthest Sun distances differ.", "Find the tilt of Uranus and its seasons; write a short article.", "Look at the textbook covers for hidden science clues (more than 25)."]),
],
takeaways=["The Earth **rotates** West to East in about **24 hours**, causing **day and night** and the apparent motion of the Sun, Moon and stars.", "The Earth **revolves** around the Sun in about **365¼ days**; the night sky changes through the year.", "**Seasons** result from the **tilt** of the axis and the spherical shape of the Earth, not from changes in distance.", "Solstices (about 21 June, 22 December) give longest and shortest days; equinoxes (about 21 March, 23 September) give equal day and night.", "**Solar eclipse:** Moon between Sun and Earth; **lunar eclipse:** Earth between Sun and Moon.", "Never watch a solar eclipse directly; lunar eclipses are safe to view.", "The Moon looks as big as the Sun because it is much closer."])
